{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-37523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $A$ is invertible" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-37523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =3$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-6(3) & =0 \\\\ 27-9-18 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-37523" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-37523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-37523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-37523" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-37527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n\n- ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0$\n- $(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +5)=0$\n- ${\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda =0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-37527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $B$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could  be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-37527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-37527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-37527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-37527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n- $B$ is invertible" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =1$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 1-2+1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (2)^{4}-2(2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 16-8+1 & =0 \\\\ 9 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97871" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-97871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97871" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $A$ is invertible" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-140835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-140834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A+I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{rank}(A+I)=3$\n- $\\text{Null}(A+I)={0}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A+I)\\ne 3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97945" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-97945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97945" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(B-4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{rank}(B-4I)=4$\n- $\\text{nullity}(B-4I)\\ne 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B-4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=B-4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(B-4I)\\ne 4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=B-4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B-4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-97946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-47568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A-3I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-47568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-3I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-3I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A-3I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-3I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-47568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-47568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sh