Statement II is true. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-I)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=C-I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-97960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-140838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-7I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A-7I$ is singular\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=0$\n- $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-7I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-7I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-7I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-7I$ is singular, then $(A-7I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-140840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-140840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97962" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}$\n- $A-3I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97962" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A-3I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A-3I) \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is non-singular, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97962" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-97962" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97962" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97962" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Col}(C-2I)={R}^{2}$\n- $C-2I$ is non-singular\n- $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(C-2I) & =2-\\text{nullity}(C-2I) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C-2I$ is singular, then $(C-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-97972" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97972" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97972" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(C+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Null}(C+3I)={0}$\n- $C+3I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C+3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(C+3I) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C+3I)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C+3I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C+3I$ is singular, then $(C+3I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-97975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(B-I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Col}(B-I)={R}^{3}$\n- $B-I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =1$ is not an eigenvalue of $B$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(B-I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(B-I) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(B-I)\\ne 3,$ according to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(B-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B-I$ is singular, then $(B-I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "077a5b9cb926808055d0cafd3c69e88749e961d3bce150103fb21b1dc4555b05"} {"question" "q-97970" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97970" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "q-97970" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/state.json" "file_sha256" "f1a21aa134e7e622caf149b06d55cbf070838d0c049dc68e8bdde6e018375527"} {"question" "e-5497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\n- $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n\n- $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/example-5497.json" "file_sha256" "a160a050f43f9e2d09fdc593360ac6b86dc5848e2df202af42fbe6ede7a16d28"} {"question" "e-5497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/example-5497.json" "file_sha256" "a160a050f43f9e2d09fdc593360ac6b86dc5848e2df202af42fbe6ede7a16d28"} {"question" "e-8606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{ \\mathbf{0}\\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/example-8606.json" "file_sha256" "2892a2cac2f128a3f1601ceada0b4388402c3b50c82d8b726975b50e67e385e4"} {"question" "e-8606" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/assets/fd48d2627c161d3e82529aeeb42bcd830c2fc83a9c8d0b7522d1857f099d6f38.png)\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/example-8606.json" "file_sha256" "2892a2cac2f128a3f1601ceada0b4388402c3b50c82d8b726975b50e67e385e4"} {"question" "e-5498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n\n- $B +4I$ is singular\n\n- $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/example-5498.json" "file_sha256" "3cf1dab3d12d02419f3ceac9ae560b7eb6312ec588b04817cddb2bdc36bbf738"} {"question" "e-5498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/assets/6273b311b461cc16f516a4d9da6d928643e988c7847cfd14e23401a265d9e25b.png)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071513/example-5498.json" "file_sha256" "3cf1dab3d12d02419f3ceac9ae560b7eb6312ec588b04817cddb2bdc36bbf738"}] "usage" {"q-47568" [] "q-37523" [] "e-8606" [] "e-5497" [] "e-5498" []} "basis" 2558 "retractions" [[17592186124992 :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $λ^{3} - λ^{2} - 6λ = 0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $λ = 3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n2. $λ = 0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n3. $A$ is invertible"] [17592186125081 :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix and $λ = 3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(A - 3I)x = 0$ has a nonzero solution\n2. $Col(A - 3I) = R^{2}$\n3. $nullity(A - 3I) = 0$"] [17592186124255 :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A$?\n\n1. $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n2. $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n3. $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$"] [17592186124255 :question/worked-solution "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A$. As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A$. Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A$. Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186124335 :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n2. $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n3. $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$"] [17592186124335 :question/worked-solution "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M$, we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n> $M$ is invertible $Col(M) = R^{3}; dim(Col(M)) = 3 = rank(M); Null(M) = {0}; dim(Null(M)) = 0 = nullity(M)$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I$.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$, then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A$, which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n$\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3$.\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n$\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186124256 :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n2. $B +4I$ is singular\n3. $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$"] [17592186124256 :question/worked-solution "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M$, we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n> $M$ is invertible $Col(M) = R^{4}; dim(Col(M)) = 4 = rank(M); Null(M) = {0}; dim(Null(M)) = 0 = nullity(M)$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem,\n$nullity(B + 4I)|= 4 - rank(B + 4I); = 4 - 3; = 1$.\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$.\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0$, we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B$. As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122975 17592186122976 17592186122977 17592186122978 17592186122979 17592186123509 17592186123510 17592186123511 17592186123512 17592186123513] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122974 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122974 :answer-field/id] [17592186122974 :answer-field/key] [17592186122974 :answer-field/type] [17592186123508 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123508 :answer-field/id] [17592186123508 :answer-field/key] [17592186123508 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
